{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789333042,"nonce":"GHmBJXktcnBiaW+lSfNpWmMyC9o=","title":"Baseline compile of a module body: the interpreter waits for the whole body","message":"bench/method_call.wren (the CI row) runs 2M calls in ~28 ms on the NUC.\nThe module body has two loops; its baseline compile took ~10 ms\n(Cranelift compiles each OSR entry as a full clone of the function, and\nstage one was the body plus the hot loop's entry), during which the\ninterpreter kept running the loop. The LLVM tier's compile of the same\nbody takes ~57 ms (opt 27, codegen 28) and never lands within the run,\nso the benchmark is 40% interpreter and 60% baseline code; nothing the\ntop tier does shows in that row.\n\nA module body runs once, so its own entry is never taken. Stage one is\nnow the hot loop's entry with a body that hands its arguments to the\ninterpreter (OsrSelect::StubBody, wren_run_interpreted); the other\nentries follow as before. Mac: stage one 8.6 -\u003e 4.7 ms, the benchmark\n26 -\u003e 22.5 ms.\n\nAlso the Cranelift field-kind note stores only when the byte changes,\nand a splice whose receiver class is known reads the byte at compile\ntime and elides the note once it records the kind (the same as the\nLLVM tier): 3590 -\u003e 3266 lines of CLIF for the module body.\n\nLeft: Cranelift OSR entries as clones (k loops = k+1 body compiles);\nthe LLVM codegen cost for large bodies (17 ms on the Mac for 2k lines\nof IR at every codegen level above O0).","files":null}]}